rings is a serious sad  and adult story with many themes that  are far too intense for children so why  the difference well tolkien himself has  the answer to this in a letter to his  publisher he wrote the generally  different tone and style of the hobbit  is due and point of genesis to it being  taken by me as a matter from the great  cycle susceptible of treatment as a  fairy story for children in another  letter written to a friend tolkien  addresses the subject of tone yet again  by saying that the hobbit was unhappily  really meant as far as i was conscious  as a children's story it has some of the  silliness of manner caught unthinkingly  from the kind of stuff i had had served  to me i deeply regret them so do  intelligent children the distinct  difference in tone between each story is  very real and even the author was aware  of the change to the point that he  outright regretted it  when tolkien went to write a sequel to  the hobbit he settled on the one ring as  the connection point between the two  stories this led to a lot of subsequent  character development and a few  adjustments to the original story for  example the mysterious necromancer  character from the hobbit was chosen to  play a much bigger role in the new story  of course that character is sauron but  what is he doing in mirkwood in a  ghostly non-physical form during the  events of the hobbit the answer lies in  tolkien's personal obsession with world  building by the time the hobbit was  published the author had already been  working on what would eventually become  the silmarillion for two previous  decades in fact and when he actually sat  down to write the hobbit he found that  his then private silmarillion world  began to encroach on the story of bilbo  baggins in one letter tolkien says that  mr baggins began as a comic tale among  conventional and inconsistent grimm's  fairy tale dwarves and got drawn into  the edge of it so that even sauron the  terrible peeped over the edge trolls are  a fascinating aspect of middle earth's  history in a letter written 17 years  after the release of the hobbit tolkien  addresses the troll conundrum saying  simply i'm not sure about trolls there  are many different versions of these  underdeveloped creatures found in  tolkien's work from hill trolls to cave  trolls to oleg hai the fierce breed of  orc-like trolls that appear in the  appendix to the return of the king the  appendices also suggest that very few of  these trolls actually speak stating in  their beginning these creatures were of  dull and lumpish nature and had no more  language than beasts but sauron had made  use of them teaching them what little  they could learn and increasing their  wits with wickedness in that  aforementioned letter tolkien breaks  down the idea that trolls are likely  only counterfeits essentially that they  were made in mockery of the ants tolkien  subsequently explains that while trolls  may be able to talk they are generally  animalistic in nature but where do the  trolls from the hobbit fit into all this  they have very english names they live a  somewhat civil lifestyle and they can  easily talk  an oversized squibble i'm a burger ah  pop it out  there  well it turns out that the only trolls  who are mentioned in the appendices as  being particularly articulate are the  stone trolls who apparently spoke a  debased form of the common speech even  then this is another area in which  tolkien's opinions changed a lot over  time and yes unfortunately that means  there's no solid answer to this one  giants are rare and mystical creatures  even for middle earth they're mentioned  throughout the lord of the rings but  only in passing in the return of the  king ministereth is described as looking  as if it had been built by giants out of  the stones of the earth in the hobbit  they're brought up as creatures in many  of gandalf's stories and bilbo thinks of  them as a potential answer during his  riddle game with gollum and then of  course there's that time when bilbo  gandalf and the dwarves travel over the  misty mountains and see some real stone  giants during a thunderstorm the stone  giants cause a considerable ruckus  laughing and throwing rocks at each  other and then proceed to never be a  viable presence in anything else that  tolkien ever writes this one's tough  since tolkien himself seems to have done  a 180 on giants in this world indeed  there are many other fairly  well-developed forms of giants to be  found in the earlier versions of  tolkien's mythos but over time the  author appears to have toned down his  interest in that race to the point where  they don't feature heavily in any of his  other works  gollum is an odd one he lives alone  obsesses over his ring and loves to  snack on any living thing that happens  to wander near his home so why when  bilbo shows up is gollum's first thought  to start a game of riddles with his new  those acquaintances process doesn't like  arms doesn't  does a lot to plow  well first off dildo has a sword and  when gollum sees sting he makes sure to  be very polite until he figures out just  who this little hobbit is at least but  that still leaves another question why  riddles why wouldn't he just chat with  bilbo or stalk him or even sit there and  watch him it turns out that tolkien  answers this one via a quick easy to  miss line in the hobbit just before the  game begins the book reads riddles were  all gollum could think of asking them  and sometimes guessing them had been the  only game he had ever played with other  funny creatures sitting in their holes  in the long long ago this isn't just a  random interest either as riddles are  clearly taken very seriously in  middle-earth after the game it becomes  clear that bilbo knows that the riddle  game is sacred to many and that even  someone like gollum would be afraid to  cheat at one in other words riddles  aren't just a passing fancy for the  peoples of middle earth they're a very  serious matter indeed  the hobbit is full of incredible beasts  and creatures and one of the most  remarkable of all is bjorn the skin  changer in the hobbit novel it's implied  that there are many other bear to human  creatures like him too so what's the  origin of this strange group and where  do shape-shifters fit into tolkien's  world unfortunately this is another area  in which the author left little more  than hints and riddles as far as the  ability to change shapes goes that's  nothing new in middle earth the angelic  valar and mayar take on physical forms  all the time and in the fellowship of  the ring gandalf describes radagast as  quote a master of shapes and changes of  hue even sauron turns into a werewolf a  serpent and a vampire bat in the  silmarillion but for all that shifting  and changing bayorn's specific race of  skin changers remains highly mysterious  all we get is gandalf's initial  description of the fellow and the hobbit  which reads some say that he is a bear  descendant from the great and ancient  bears of the mountains that lived there  before the giants came others say that  he is a man descended from the first men  who lived before smaug or the other  dragons came into this part of the world  and before the goblins came into the  hills out of the north while gandalf  isn't sure himself he apparently favors  the second option but as he himself also  says about the matter i cannot tell you  much more though that ought to be enough  if you've read the hobbit and the lord  of the rings you might be wondering how  far the seven dwarven rings are involved  with bilbo's adventures after all the  hobbit features the arkenstone which is  a super jewel with its own inner power  the silmarillion also explains that the  foundation of each of the seven hordes  of the dwarf kings of old was a golden  ring but all those hordes long ago were  plundered and the dragons devoured them  it would seem natural then to suggest  that the lonely mountain might have been  home to one of those hordes so is there  a dwarven ring lying at the heart of the  mountain or possibly in smaug's belly as  it turns out no but kind of see the  lonely mountain is originally founded by  some of the dispossessed dwarves who are  driven out of moria by the balrok long  before that point one of their kings  receives one of the seven dwarven rings  and it stays in the family until  thorin's father loses it when he's  captured by the necromancer so while  there isn't a dwarven ring present in  erebor when bilbo arrives on the scene  one of the rings of power is likely  responsible for the mass of gold and  treasure on which maug sleeps  you've probably noticed that eagles play  an important role in tolkien's world  they say bilbo gandalf and the dwarves  from the clutches of the orcs of the  misty mountains they show up to help  turn the tide during the battle of the  five armies their leader gwyhere the  windlord saves gandalf when he's trapped  on orthak and his eagles swoop in to  join in the battle at the black gate of  mordor too eagles also saved the day  multiple times during the silmarillion  so what's up with the eagles always  arriving in the nick of time is it a  cop-out or a lazy excuse to resolve a  story  nope tolkien's use of the eagles to  provide dramatic savior-like moments is  something that he developed specifically  and intentionally in fact he coined a  term for this plot device a eu  catastrophe in tolkien's famous essay on  fairy stories the author points out that  while drama and tragedy go hand in hand  fairy stories must come with a  consolation or happy ending in a letter  written to his son christopher the  author defines the concept of a eu  catastrophe as the sudden happy turn in  a story which pierces you with the joy  that brings tears which i argued is the  highest function of fairy stories to  produce when writing fantasy tolkien  felt that the story must provide these  moments of grace and salvation and the  majestic eagles remained one of his  favorite ways of doing so  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

